ormance Efficiency:** t3.small instances provide double the throughput of t3.micro (80 vs. 40 req/s) at double per-instance cost. This right-sizing directly addresses peak-load sustainability (N1). With 50% CloudFront cache hit rate (N1), the origin tier avoids serving 750 req/s directly; without caching, sustaining 1,500 req/s would require ~19 t3.small instances. The proposed ASG max of 10 instances balances elasticity with cost constraints: sufficient spare capacity to handle demand spikes smoothly, and sufficient headroom to scale down to the minimum of 2 during steady state. Using t3.micro to meet peak demand would require 19–20 instances at $15.19 × (19/2) per month in compute alone, exceeding the N5 budget of $750 immediately.\n\n**Cost Optimization:** Original BOM cost calculation (price sheet values): EC2 t3.micro 1460 h × $0.0104 = $15.19; ALB 730 h × $0.0225 = $16.43; ALB LCU 1460 h × $0.008 = $11.68; NAT hour 730 h × $0.045 = $32.85; NAT GB 100 × $0.045 = $4.50; RDS hour Multi-AZ (730 h × $0.017 × 2.0) = $24.82; RDS storage Multi-AZ (20 GB × $0.115 × 2.0) = $4.60; S3 50 × $0.023 = $1.15; CloudFront GB 200 × $0.085 = $17.00; CloudFront requests 500 × $0.01 = $5.00; Total = $533.30. Proposed BOM cost: EC2 t3.small 1460 h × $0.0208 = $30.37; ALB $16.43; ALB LCU $11.68; NAT hour $32.85; NAT GB 50 × $0.045 = $2.25; RDS hour Multi-AZ (1460 h × $0.017 × 2.0) = $49.64; RDS storage Multi-AZ (40 GB × $0.115 × 2.0) = $9.20; S3 $1.15; CloudFront GB 2000 × $0.085 = $170.00; CloudFront requests 4000 × $0.01 = $40.00; Total = $363.57 + $370.58 = $734.15. The proposed cost remains within N5 budget of $750, with $15.85 contingency.",
